    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here is a potential teaching guide on how systemic racism operates in housing and banking systems:\n\n**Module Title:** \"Systemic Racism in Housing and Banking: Understanding how Inequality is Perpetuated\"\n\n**Introduction:** Systemic racism refers to the way in which racism is deeply ingrained in institutions and systems, resulting in unequal outcomes for different racial groups. In this module, we will explore how systemic racism operates in housing and banking systems, highlighting how these systems perpetuate inequality and limit access to resources for marginalized communities.\n\n**Housing System:**\n\n* Describe how redlining, a practice of denying homeownership to low-income communities of color, still exists today in the form of \"sigmoidal maps\" used by financial institutions to assess neighborhood risk.\n* Explain how discriminatory policies and lending practices, such as \"steering,\" steer people away from neighborhoods with affordable housing and towards gentrified areas, further exacerbating racial segregation.\n* Discuss how zoning laws and racially discriminatory practices have historically limited access to quality education, healthcare, and employment opportunities for marginalized communities.\n\n**Banking System:**\n\n* Describe the concept of the \"collateral gap,\" which makes it difficult for people of color to access mortgages due to lower credit scores and deposits, despite similar",
